      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. Its declarative form is an explicit avowal, regardless of whether that avowal is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows present love but does not itself promise future conduct, assume a new obligation, or renew a specified undertaking. Lear's decision to distribute bounty in response to declarations of love does not convert Goneril's avowal into a commitment by her.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression is part of an elaborate, formally solicited declaration rather than a reflex-like or affectively discharged response. The surrounding rhetoric about words failing remains a composed assertion and does not establish expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression functions principally as an avowal answering Lear's question. Its competitive and persuasive setting affects how the avowal may be interpreted, but does not establish a separate core force beyond T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to the extent of his bounty. Goneril answers first, addressing Lear with the focal avowal as the opening of a sustained declaration of exceptional filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no definite action or obligation in the focal expression. Lear accepts the declaration sufficiently to grant her territory, but that practical response is uptake of her professed degree of love, not evidence that the focal words constituted a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare's King Lear. No facts beyond the supplied evidence were used.\",\"limitations\":\"The scene supplies a strong strategic incentive for Goneril's declaration and Cordelia privately contrasts her own love with her sisters' speech, but the supplied passage does not conclusively establish whether Goneril actually feels any love, how sincere she is, or what conduct she intends.\"}"
        }
